content.content:
{
  "fields": {
    "schema-version": "1"
  },
  "item-count": 6,
  "schema-version": 1,
  "sections": {
    "context": "`MemoryStore` (`src/store.ts`) already bounds *how much history* a series\nholds — `maxPerSeries` caps each bucket, and `prune` expires anything past\nretention. Neither bounds *how many series* exist. `put` calls\n`this.buckets.set(sample.series, bucket)` for any series name it has not\nseen before, with no ceiling on the number of distinct keys the `Map` can\nhold. `validateSample` (`src/validate.ts`) only checks that a series name\nis well-formed (`[a-z][a-z0-9_.-]*`), not that it is one of a known or\nexpected set — so a caller that mints a fresh label per request (a stray\nrequest id, a UUID, a bug in a client) grows `buckets` without limit until\nthe process runs out of memory. This is the cardinality half of the bound\nthe ingest queue and per-series cap already cover on the volume half.\n\nNon-goals: this does not change per-series retention or the\n`maxPerSeries` cap, add a durable/backing store, add authentication or\nvalidation of what a series name is allowed to *mean*, or evict an\nalready-tracked series to make room for a new one — the chosen bound\nrejects the *new* series instead of destroying an existing one's held\nhistory (see the accompanying ADR for why). It also does not change wire\nformat or the public `Router` HTTP surface.",
    "criteria": [
      {
        "id": "store-construction-takes-a-maxseries",
        "statement": "`MemoryStore`'s constructor takes a `maxSeries` cap (alongside the\nexisting `maxPerSeries`) and throws `RangeError` if it is not a\npositive integer, mirroring the existing `maxPerSeries` check.",
        "title": "store construction takes a maxSeries cap"
      },
      {
        "id": "brand-new-series-is",
        "statement": "When the store already holds `maxSeries` distinct series, calling\n`put` with a sample whose series is not already tracked does not\ncreate a new bucket and does not insert the sample.",
        "title": "a brand-new series is rejected once the store is at capacity"
      },
      {
        "id": "already-tracked-series-keeps",
        "statement": "When the store is at `maxSeries` distinct series, `put` with a\nsample for a series that IS already tracked still inserts normally —\nthe cap bounds cardinality, not per-series throughput.",
        "title": "an already-tracked series keeps accepting samples at capacity"
      },
      {
        "id": "rejected-for-cardinality-samples-are",
        "statement": "The store exposes a counter of samples dropped because the series\ncap was reached, distinct from `IngestQueue.droppedCount()`, so an\noperator can tell a cardinality-limit hit from ordinary queue\noverflow.",
        "title": "rejected-for-cardinality samples are counted"
      },
      {
        "id": "cap-is-configurable-via",
        "statement": "A new `ROLLUP_MAX_SERIES` environment variable sets the cap\n(mirroring `ROLLUP_MAX_SAMPLES`), with a positive-integer default in\n`DEFAULT_CONFIG`, validated the same way as the other env-read\nconfig values (throws `ConfigError` on a present-but-unusable\nvalue).",
        "title": "the cap is configurable via the environment"
      },
      {
        "id": "startup-reporting-surfaces-the-new",
        "statement": "`main()`'s startup line includes the count of samples dropped for\nexceeding the series cap, alongside the existing series count and\nqueue-dropped count.",
        "title": "startup reporting surfaces the new counter"
      }
    ],
    "goal": "Cap the number of distinct series `MemoryStore` will hold at once, so a\nsingle runaway label cannot grow the series map without limit and exhaust\nprocess memory."
  },
  "slug": "bound-the-number-of-distinct",
  "staged": "bound-the-number-of-distinct",
  "type": "spec"
}

